 { In this section, we're going to take a look at how you can use your pawns in order to fight against your opponent's minor pieces. Common methods here range from pushing back your opponent's pieces, to using your pawns as barriers in order to prevent them from reaching more active posts. Since pawns can also get in the way of your own pieces, we'll also take a look at some common mistakes to avoid in this respect later on. Let's start things off with a quick puzzle! This position was reached in the online game Carlsen,M - Bogdan-Daniel,D (2023) . Can you spot the crushing move missed by Black? } 1... { -KEY- } g5 $1 { Correct! Black uses his pawn to push the knight back. } ( 1... Rd2 $2 { was the game continuation. However, this lets White off the hook. One possible continuation would be } 2. Qc8 Kh7 3. Qe6 $1 { when Black can win a pawn after } Qxe6 4. Nxe6 Nxf2 { but now White grabs on c7. } 5. Nxc7 $11 { The position remains complex with White's king being the more vulnerable one, but White's c6 pawn should provide enough resources to hold the balance. } ) 
2. Qxf7+ Kxf7 { Black's king and rook work together to cover the forward jumps of White's knight, forcing it to passively retreat. } 3. Nh3 { White retreats to h3 to defend the f2 pawn, but now the knight is completely out of the game. } ( 3. Ne2 $6 { Given one more move, the knight would place itself on the central outpost on d4 when the position would be fine for him, but Black is in time to kill this plan: } Rd2 $19 { White's knight is permanently tied down because of the weakness of the f2 pawn, while Black can play ...Ra2 next and begin to collect material. Black is winning. } ) 
Rd2 $19 { Black is already winning, as he is in effect playing with an extra piece. The only concern is White's passed a-pawn, but Black has calculated that he is in time to stop its advance. } 4. Ra1 Ke6 5. a4 Kd7 6. a5 Kc8 7. a6 Kb8 $19 { With the pawn unable to continue its march forward, Black can begin to target both of White's queenside pawns with his rook. Staying alert for ways to push back the opponent's pieces to passive squares is one of the fastest ways to a positional advantage. Let's take a look at a more advanced example of this next. } 
