 { This is taken from another of Magnus Carlsen's online games, with the Black player being the strong Russian GM Aleksei Sarana. It's Black to play, and the problem that he faces is that White is threatening Nd1-e3 striking at the Black rook and from there jumping the knight to either the weak d5 square, or perhaps to c4 from where it can target the d6 pawn. Can you find a move that puts a stop to this plan? } 1... { -KEY- } g5 $1 { Correct! Now White now has no time to play his desired move of Nd1-e3. } ( 1... Be6 { was played in the game. } ) 
2. Be3 { The only square for the bishop, but Nd1-e3 ideas have been stopped for now. } Rxf1+ 3. Bxf1 h6 $11 { Black defends the g5 pawn and the game hangs in the balance. While it's true that Black's kingside pawn cover is now a little bit more shaky, that is a small price to pay for having killed off White's main positional idea. So far we've seen situations where the pawns help to push back the pieces. Another common technique involves using your pawns to form a barrier to keep pieces from advancing. We'll look at this in action next. } 
