 { White has a nice position here with more space in the centre and queenside. As a consequence, Black's minor pieces are generally quite passive. The only chance for active piece play that Black has is to play the move ...Nf4. Because of this, White comes up with a strange-looking but highly effective plan. } 1. { -KEY- } Qh2 $1 { It's rare that the best move in a position would be to make your queen as passive of a piece as possible! The point however is that now White's pawn on g2 is free to step forward. } ( 1. Qg4 $6 { This square would be less accurate, as it would feed into Black's plan of advancing with ...h5 and then ...g4. } h5 $1 2. Qf5 Qg7 $13 { Suddenly White has to deal with pawn breaks as well as the possibility of having their queen trapped. The position is messy and unclear. } ) 
h5 { Black moves forward with the pawns, but now White has a strong technique available to them. } ( 1... Nf4 $2 { Now the immediate knight jump is easily refuted. } 2. g3 $1 Rg8 { Black uses tactics to maintain the knight on f4 now, with the point being that if White plays gxf4 here then after ...gxf4+ Black will get their piece back on the next move. } 3. Bd2 $16 { Now White is threatening to capture the knight. } Ng6 4. Be2 $16 { White will soon activate their queen, while none of Black's pieces are able to find good squares. Notice how it is not only the kingside pawns, but also White's pawns on the centre and queenside that are restricting Black's army. } ) 
2. g3 $1 { This method of closing the doors against enemy pawn breaks is discussed in detail in the famous book 'Techniques of Positional Play', also released on Chessable. The point is that if Black now goes for the ...g4 pawn break, then White plays h3-h4 and the positions remains closed. While if Black plays ...h4 then White can maintain the lock with g3-g4 if they'd like. } h4 ( 2... g4 $2 3. h4 $1 { This is an ideal structure for White, since the g3/h4 pawn formation keeps Black's knight on g6 unable to make further inroads into the White position. With 2...h4 Black is trying to destabilize White's kingside pawn chain. However, here White has two reasonable approaches. One option is to play g3-g4, conceding the f4 outpost but arguing that White will have no real issues playing around the knight on f4. The alternative approach is similar in strength but more in keeping with my personal prefences. } ) 
3. Be2 $1 { White keeps the pawn tension and points the bishop at the newly weakened light squares on the kingside. } Qg7 4. Qg2 $16 { The queen will eventually return to the action via f3. In the long run, White stands better due to their space advantage on the queenside, and the possibility to maneuver a piece to the f5 outpost. This kind of situation is relatively rare. A much more common way to use pawns as barriers is when targeting the enemy bishop as in our next two examples. } 
