 { One of the most common targets for pawns is Black's dark-squared fianchettoed bishop. In this position, we see that Black has gone for an aggressive opening, bringing their queen out early in the hopes of putting White's centre under pressure. The problem is that White can set up a highly effective pawn formation that will shut out the fianchettoed bishop. } 1. { -KEY- } e5 $1 { First, the knight is pushed back to a more passive square. } Nfd7 2. f4 $1 { Now White sets up a pawn barrier with f4/e5. This is extremely problematic for Black since the pawn break ...f7-f6 will always have the problem of weakening the e6 square and the a2-f7 diagonal. } Nc6 { Black focuses on development. Let's show how the game might play out over the next few moves. } ( 2... f6 $4 { is too weakening, and in this case loses material after } 3. e6 $1 { since the knight only has one square to jump to, but after } Nb6 4. Nb3 $1 Qb4 5. a3 $18 { The queen is trapped! } ) 
3. Nb3 Qc7 4. Qf3 ( { But not } 4. Nxc5 $2 { when after } Nxc5 5. Bxc5 Nxe5 $17 { Black recovers their pawn with a near-winning position owing to White's weakened king.This is the danger of the e5/f4 pawn formation - your king is left very exposed should the opponent find a way of breaking out of the cage. } ) 
b6 5. Nd5 $16 { White is clearly in control here, with long castling likely to come next and the potential to attack on the kingside with the h4-h5 pawn break. Sometimes, the path to blocking your opponent's bishop requires less aggressive-looking measures than this and can be done by way of a simple central pawn exchange, as in our next example. } 
