 { This London System position was reached by move 6 in an online game. GM Vugar Rasulov has just played ...c5, challenging White's central control and inviting a change in structure. } 7. { -KEY- } dxc5 $1 { The exchange of pawns condemns Black's fianchettoed bishop to a passive role. } ( 7. Be2 $6 { A mechanical move such as this one would on the other hand have given Black all the chances to fight for an edge: } Qb6 8. Qc1 cxd4 $1 9. exd4 e5 $1 { At first glance this seems like a pawn sacrifice, but in fact White won't have time to win any material. } 10. dxe5 Nd5 11. Bh2 dxe5 12. O-O ( 12. Bxe5 $2 { Grabbing the pawn with White's king uncastled would be a recipe for disaster. } Bxe5 13. Nxe5 Re8 $17 { White will struggle to survive, with no plan available in order to castle without losing material. } ) 
Nc6 $15 { Black has the better chances due to their better central control and well-placed pieces. } ) 
dxc5 { With the d-pawns off the board, we can see that White's f4 bishop has gained activity, while the b2/c3 pawn chain makes it difficult for its dark-squared counterpart to play a significant role in the middlegame battle ahead. Over the coming moves, White goes about setting up the ideal piece/pawn configuration. } 8. Nbd2 Nc6 9. Qc2 Bf5 10. e4 $1 Be6 11. Be2 $16 { White reaches the ideal arrangement. Black's position is uncomfortable as both of his bishops are struggling for ideal squares. In the same way that bishops can be effectively blocked by enemy pawns, they can also find themselves blocked by friendly pawns. Let's study a couple of examples of this next. } 
