1... { -KEY- } e5 $2 { In their eagerness to fight for the centre, Black mistakenly puts one of their own pawns in front of their dark-squared bishop. } ( 1... Nxe4 $1 { The tactical approach should have been preferred, when after } 2. Nxe4 d5 $11 { Black recovers their piece while reducing the amount of material on the board, helpful when you are the side with a little less space. } ) 
2. dxe5 $1 { White jumps at the chance to lock the pawn structure in the centre. } ( 2. h3 $6 { Keeping the central pawn tension would be a significant mistake, allowing Black to keep their dark-squared bishop active: } exd4 $1 3. Nxd4 Re8 $1 { Suddenly it's not easy to defend the pawn on e4. This is where mistakes quickly creep in. For example: } 4. f3 $2 { Now the bishop on e3 is undefended. } Na5 5. Bd3 d5 $17 { Suddenly Black is the one with the initiative, with their fianchettoed bishop remaining a powerful piece. } ) 
Nxe5 ( { Forced, since } 2... dxe5 $2 { runs into tactical problems: } 3. Bc5 $1 Re8 4. Ng5 $1 { There is no good way to defend the f7 pawn. } Be6 { A sad necessity. } 5. Nxe6 fxe6 6. Bb5 $16 { White will capture on c6 next, further crippling Black's structure. } ) 
3. Nxe5 $1 { White continues the plan of forcing a fixed central structure. } dxe5 { The effect of the exchanges can be seen in the difference between the dark-squared bishops. } 4. Qxd8 Rxd8 5. Rfd1 { Even though Black was the first to arrive onto the d-file, a surprising problem appears for them. In order to hold the file, they need to develop their light-squared bishop. But the more active squares of e6 and g4 are too well-guarded by the opponent! } Bd7 { Black has nothing better than to step to the passive d7 square. } ( 5... Bg4 $6 6. f3 { and the bishop is kicked back. } ) 
 ( 5... Be6 $6 6. Bxe6 $16 { Black's structure is heavily damaged. } ) 
6. f3 $14 { White has a small but stable plus based on their superior minor pieces. } 
