 ( { In this position, which move do you think is best between } 1. d3 { or } 1. d4 $2 ) 
16. { -KEY- } d3 $1 { Correct! With this move White keeps the advantage, since not only do they stop Black from trading off the bishop pair but they also keep the a1-h8 diagonal to be used by their dark-squared bishop. } ( 16. d4 $2 { After this move White's dark-squared bishop will never exert pressure along the a1-h8 diagonal. To make matters worse, it is now Black to play and they have a strong positional idea! } Bf5 $1 { Forcing off White's bishop pair. } 17. Qc3 Bxb1 $15 { Not only has White been robbed of the bishop pair, but they are likely facing a good knight vs bad bishop dynamic due to the majority of their pawns being on dark squares. } ) 
Bf5 17. Bd2 Rfe8 18. Bc3 $16 { White's bishop arrives on the long diagonal, and with it White sets up the better middlegame chances. Note also that the d3 pawn does a good job of restricting the forward jumps of Black's knight by controlling the c4 square. The lesson here is that it's important to make use of ways in which you can restrict your opponent's pieces with your pawns, but it's equally important to make sure that you don't restrict your own pieces! We'll now close out the section with two instructive sequences. } 
