 { White had just played 10.Nd5?! in the game Winter,W - Capablanca,J (1919) . From here Black played what was to become a famous positional sequence broken up into several strategic steps. } 10... { -KEY- } g5 $1 { Step 1: Break the pin, and force the bishop's retreat to the passive g3 square. } 11. Nxf6+ ( 11. Bg3 $2 { is similar to the game but worse, since after } Nxd5 $1 12. exd5 f6 $17 { Black builds an impregnable barrier along the dark squares, leaving White's bishop sidelined on the g3 square. } ) 
 ( 11. Nxg5 $2 { This tactical idea fails to } Nxd5 $19 { since after } 12. exd5 hxg5 $19 { Black goes up a piece. } ( { Black should avoid } 11. Nxf6+ hxg5 { because of } 12. Bxg5 Be7 13. Nxe7 Qxe7 14. Qf3 Kg7 15. Qg3 $16 { when White is the side that is pressing, with a lot of attacking possibilities for only a small amount of sacrificed material. } ) 
 ) 
Qxf6 12. Bg3 { White's bishop no longer has any squares to move to, but he can still hope to bring it back into the game by retreating his knight and then playing f2-f3. } Bg4 $1 { Step 2: Pin the f3 knight to the queen. } 13. h3 Bxf3 $1 { Step 3: Give up the bishop pair to cripple White's structure and ensure that the f2 and f3 pawns will be unable to move for the remainder of the game. } 14. Qxf3 Qxf3 15. gxf3 f6 $1 { Step 4: In the final step, Black completes the bunker-like formation of pawns, ensuring maximum stability and control over the critical f4 square. The point is that so long as White can never play f3-f4, then they have no pathway to activate their bishop! And as long as this is so, then Black is effectively a piece to the good! From here on out, Capablanca instructively converts his advantage by shifting the focus to the queenside. } 16. Kg2 a5 17. a4 Kf7 18. Rh1 Ke6 { Centralizing the king. } 19. h4 Rfb8 { Preparing to open lines on the queenside. } 20. hxg5 hxg5 21. b3 c6 22. Ra2 b5 { The first pawn break. } 23. Rha1 c4 $1 { The second pawn break! } 24. axb5 cxb3 $1 { White's queenside structure crumbles. } 25. cxb3 Rxb5 26. Ra4 Rxb3 ( 26... c5 $1 { Shutting down d3-d4 possibilities was a little more precise. } ) 
27. d4 Rb5 28. Rc4 $2 ( { The final mistake, } 28. dxe5 { offered better hopes for survival. } ) 
Rb4 $1 29. Rxc6 Rxd4 $19 { Black is up a pawn while White's bishop remains cemented. White resigned. } 
 0-1

