 { Our final instructive sequence is taken from the game Collins,S - Trevelyan,J (2005) . The position is objectively equal here, but from here on out White uses his pawns to great effect, progressively restricting Black's knight and eventually carving out a winning advantage. } 17. { -KEY- } Re1 Ne6 $6 { The first inaccuracy. } ( 17... b5 $1 { Black needed to be careful here and play a move such as this one. The reason is a subtle one. Since White has the light-squared bishop, he is seeking to arrange his pawns on dark squares, including a pawn on b4. Black therefore begins advancing his pawns to contest this square. } 18. a3 Rab8 19. c3 { White might try c2-c3, with the idea of playing Kc2 and eventually b2-b4. However, Black is first to the party: } ( 18... Ne6 18. b4 $2 { is too dangerous since now Black can play } Na4 $15 { when the c3 and c4 squares are too weak for White to claim any advantage. } ) 
a5 $1 20. Kc2 b4 $11 { With the b2-b4 advance permanently stopped, Black is not in any danger. } ) 
18. Re3 { White lifts his rook with the possibility of transferring his rook to c3 or b3 at an opportune moment. } Nd4 { The knight lands on the central square, but as it is not an outpost, it will soon find itself pushed back. } 19. c3 Rac8 20. b4 $1 { A very nice move. White frees the b2 square for his king, while seizing control over the c5 square at the same time. If we compare this position to a few moves ago, we can see that Black has gained nothing from his maneuvering. The knight was much better placed on c5 in our starting position than it is on d4. } Nc6 $2 { The knight is not well-placed here as all its forward jumps are controlled by the White pawns. } 21. Kb2 a5 { Black tries to fight for the b4 square, but by now it is firmly under White's grip. } 22. a3 $1 { Maintaing the ideal pawn formation to keep Black's knight restricted. } axb4 23. axb4 Ra8 { The rook transfers to the newly opened file, hoping to rustle up some counterplay. However, an important concept when battling for open files is that they are typically only valuable if you can eventually find an entry square. White shows that here this is not the case. } 24. Bf1 $1 Ra7 25. Re1 Rda8 26. Bc4 { White has secured control of both entry points, and can soon look to target Black's backward pawn on d6. } Rd8 27. Red1 { The end of the sequence that we're interested in, with White already having a decisive positional advantage. I've included the full game for any student who is curious to see how play unfolded. } Raa8 28. R5d2 Ra7 29. Kb3 Rda8 30. Kb2 Rd8 31. Bd5 Rda8 32. f4 { Much like Capablanca in the previous game, White needs to open some new lines of attack in order to impose their positional advantage. } Rd8 33. Rf2 Nb8 34. Bb3 Nd7 35. Rfd2 Nf8 36. Rf2 Ne6 $2 { Under pressure for a long time, Black makes the final mistake. } 37. fxe5 fxe5 ( { If } 37... dxe5 { then after } 38. Rdf1 { there is no way to save the f6 pawn. } ) 
38. Rdf1 Rf8 39. Rf6 $18 { White is threatening both Rxh6 and Bxe6, since after the latter ...fxe6 will lose a rook after Rxf8. } Ng5 40. Bd5 { White has too many threats, and so Black resigned. I hope you enjoyed this section, which has been all about using the pawns to fight against pieces. In the final section of the chapter we'll be showing the power of pawn majorities, in particular the dangerous 4 vs 3 majority. } 
